Z||000000||ig88vsbobafett||12-31-2001||10:35 AM||||OK so far i have come up with<BR>Remove the cat. conveter<BR>get a K&N Filter ( Where can i get them)<BR>New Performance cam (Is it worth the $170 at fierostore) how much Hp dose it add ?<P>iam at trying to do this with out boring the motor headers ??? do they have them for the 2.5? i have a 84 so a v6 will be realy hard form what i hear<P>i am looking to get 120to130 hp out of a 92hp duke<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Dave Gunsul||12-31-2001||11:19 AM||||I would go with a performance cam from either Comp. Cams or Crane. They both make performance cams for the duke and they offer<BR>a lot more choices and power ranges then the Fiero store does. Try: <A HREF="http://www.compcams.com" TARGET=_blank>www.compcams.com</A> <BR>or: <A HREF="http://www.cranecams.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cranecams.com</A> <P>2. Get the big bore t-body from the Fiero store.<BR>3. Holley still makes the header for the duke<BR>part#'s 4702 for the 1-3/4 primary long tube<BR>header ( this is for someone that cuts the cat off like you did) or 4703 for the header that accomodates the factory cat.<BR>4. From what I understand you can have your head machined to accept big blcok chevy roller rocker arms. This will cut some friction down and add some power. If you switched to a roller cam and had these roller rockers you would be able to rev. the duke up a little more and it would last a lot longer. The 85 and 86 dukes have roller cams and still use all the same stuff as yours does so you might want to look into using one of these blocks instead. The Fiero store is developing performance roller cams for the roller dukes so you would still be able to get a performance cam for it if they get it done in time.<BR>5. If you can find one get the Holly or Edelbrock manifold that was made for the duke. These are kind-a hard to find but they show up on ebay sometimes. You could also have your stock manifold ported instead.<BR>6. Port the head or buy an aluminum SD head from GM performance parts. Obviously the alum. head is going to cost you a good penny<BR>so don't be shocked if you go that route.<BR>Porting a stock head would cost less and the Fiero shop in canada already sells a ported head for $275.00 complete. You would have to change the valve springs to match whatever cam you decided to use but otherwise you'd be good to go.<BR>7. I would highly suggest that you use some safety items to help keep the engine together such as: high vol. oil pump, oil cooler, ARP bolts, a very long break in period, maybe even a dual oil filter system so you'll have a little more oil.<BR>8. A performance coil and MSD box wouldn't hurt either. They make the throttle very sensitive and even help idle and gas mileage so you just can't lose with this stuff.<P>Whew, sorry for the length but I hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>Activities Director<BR>Northern IL. Fiero Enthusiasts<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000002||ig88vsbobafett||12-31-2001||01:51 PM||||Hi i found this site that tells you how to get 121 to 180 Hp form a duke <BR> <A HREF="http://www.amotion.com/p4.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.amotion.com/p4.html</A> <P>I am thinking of doing this any idea where i can get the parts and where to get it done <P><BR>    RV AND COMMUTER  (1000 rpm to 4000 rpm, max. HP @ 5000 rpm)<BR>Good fuel economy and improved mid-range performance for every day use.<BR>No internal engine modifications are required.<BR>ENGINE          CAM RANGE*            AIR/FUEL RANGE      HORSEPOWER<BR>151            19600 to 20400         222 to  278 cfm     Max. 121 HP<BR>              *The cam range refers to the 2nd half of<BR>               the Accelerated Motion part number.<BR>Compression:   Gasoline 9.5:1 or less, Propane 11.0:1 or less<BR>Exhaust:       Stock or small tube headers<BR>Gear Ratio:    3.9:1 or lower (numerically)<BR>Idle:          850 rpm or less<BR>Ignition:      Recurved distributor and electronic ignition<P>||||reg||1
